00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.910
This is Heathrow Airport—Britain’s Busiest.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:16.619
Each day, about 650 flights take off from
one of its two runways.

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:21.769
At the busiest times, this represents one
aircraft every 45 seconds.

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:26.800
Of those 650, about twenty flights daily head
to Frankfurt.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.519
Between British Airways and Lufthansa, flights
on this route depart more than hourly throughout

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:32.590
the daytime.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.980
This makes Frankfurt one of Heathrow’s most
frequently served destinations.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.280
Despite the unremarkable nature of such a
flight, the complexity of even this one hour

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.899
hop is enormous.

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:47.780
It takes dozens of people spread out all across
the continent to coordinate and navigate just

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.000
one of these flights safely to its destination.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.030
This is everything that happens within the
90 minutes it takes to for a plane to get

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.940
from its gate at Heathrow to its gate in Frankfurt.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:03.130
Hours before the flight is scheduled to take
off, British Airways will have sent a flight

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:08.220
plan to here—Eurocontrol’s Network Manager
Operations Centre in Brussels.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.510
Now, Eurocontrol is an intergovernmental air
traffic management organization.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:17.680
“Prissinotti: [00:01:57] Well, Eurocontrol
is an intergovernmental organization of 43

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:23.070
states in Europe and beyond and we do air
traffic management service transversal.”

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:27.211
What that means is that Eurocontrol deals
with a variety of different aspects of the

00:01:27.211 --> 00:01:30.180
job of managing air traffic in Europe.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.390
One element of that is what’s called network
management.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.390
This function essentially involves, from this
room, making sure that flights make their

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.180
way through Europe as safely and smoothly
as possible.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.800
That field is headed up by Lacopo Prissinotti—Eurocontrol’s
Director of Network Management.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.490
“Prissinotti: [00:05:53] So here you see
that's our operational room.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:57.950
The scope is to provide services to 43 air
navigation service providers, to provide services

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:05.560
to more than 500 airports, and to provide
services to more than 1000 airlines over the

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:06.560
network.”

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.850
One corner of this room is devoted to checking
those flight plans that airlines send in.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:14.720
Now, what they need to check is that these
plans are following the rules.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.160
You see, to aircraft, the sky does not look
like this, but rather this.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.630
At least in most of Europe, there are thousands
upon thousands of pre-defined airways each

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:29.640
with their own rules on directionality and
routes and more so aircraft not only need

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.410
to fly on these roads but they also need to
follow the rules of the roads.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.250
Looking at this map, you can see why, for
example, you won’t see many planes flying

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:43.030
over this area in the east of England—there
just aren’t many airways there.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.640
Eurocontrol sends the fight plans through
a program to check that they follows these

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.850
rules but if the computer rejects it, then
it goes to these people in about 2% of cases

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.900
who coordinate with the airline to fix it
manually.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.450
From there, they’ll distribute the flight
plans to all the air traffic control centers

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.740
that each aircraft is expected to fly over.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.320
That process happens before the flight has
even taken off.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.650
Going back to that British Airways, Heathrow
to Frankfurt flight, once their plan is approved,

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:13.459
the plane is fueled, loaded, and ready to
go, the pilot will get approval from British

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:18.340
Airways’ flight dispatcher and Heathrow’s
ground control to push back.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.600
Ground control at Heathrow is responsible
for navigating all the vehicles and planes

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.350
safely across the apron up until when they
reach the runway.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:29.470
The moment an aircraft gets to the runway,
they are then the responsibility of tower

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:33.710
control which, assuming all is well, clears
them for takeoff.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:37.459
As the plane reaches altitude, it will be
passed off to the London Terminal Control

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:43.440
Centre located near Southampton which navigates
aircraft through the complex London-area airspace

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.750
until they reach 24,500 feet, or flight level
245, which for this flight should be just

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.240
about when it reaches the coast.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.000
From there, they’ll be transferred to the
London Area Control Centre, which is physically

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.650
located in the same building, to navigate
them across the channel.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:04.209
Once they reach about halfway across, however,
they leave the UK’s airspace and enter Belgium’s.

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:09.410
With that, they are now the responsibility
of the Maastricht Upper Area Control Center—also

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:10.410
managed by Eurocontrol.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.630
“Santurbano: [00:00:20] We are a European
nonprofit.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:18.609
A cross-border military civil air navigation
service provider.

00:04:18.609 --> 00:04:25.260
And, uh, yeah, our job is that we handle safely
and in an efficient and performed way all

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.930
traffic above flight level 245—24,500 feet.”

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:37.310
To reiterate, they handle traffic across this
whole area, above 24,500 feet or 7,500 meters.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:45.039
“Santurbano: [00:01:45] So we manage, what,
more or less 1.9 Million of movements per

00:04:45.039 --> 00:04:52.340
year, so between 5,000 and 5,700 movements
depending on the on the season and the day,

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.340
per day.”

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.849
It’s one of the busiest and most complex
airspaces in Europe especially as it receives

00:04:57.849 --> 00:05:02.660
a significant amount of traffic climbing from
or descending into four of of Europe’s five

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:07.189
busiest airports—Heathrow, Schiphol, Frankfurt,
and Paris Charles de Gaulle.

00:05:07.189 --> 00:05:12.030
Every single one of those flights, as long
as it’s between flight level 245 and 660,

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.559
is in contact with the people in this room.

00:05:14.559 --> 00:05:19.830
Now, the Maastricht control centre’s airspace
is divided into three sector groups—the

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.919
Brussels, Hannover, and DECO sector groups.

00:05:22.919 --> 00:05:27.550
Each sector group is staffed by their own
set of controllers who only work on their

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.569
group—a Brussels sector group controller
would very rarely switch over to the Hannover

00:05:31.569 --> 00:05:32.650
group, for example.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:38.159
Many controllers spend their entire careers
working at one control center within one sector

00:05:38.159 --> 00:05:39.159
group.

00:05:39.159 --> 00:05:42.710
This allows them to really learn the design
of their airspace in depth.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:46.259
Each of the sector groups is then divided
into sectors themselves.

00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:50.610
Now, sectors can be divided both horizontally
and vertically.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:55.830
For example, there’s the Luxembourg sector,
between flight level 245 and 355, and also

00:05:55.830 --> 00:06:00.060
the Luxembourg High sector, above flight level
335.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:05.699
At the very busiest times, each sector will
have its own dedicated set of controllers.

00:06:05.699 --> 00:06:10.800
At less busy times, though, they can and do
combine the upper and lower sector together

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.599
so they’re staffed by one set of controllers.

00:06:13.599 --> 00:06:17.430
At the least busy times, such as in the middle
of the night, they’ll often combine a number

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:18.530
of sectors.

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:23.180
For example, the entirety of Belgian airspace
is typically controlled by one set of controllers

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.610
in the dead of night.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.309
In charge of each sector are two controllers
working as a team.

00:06:28.309 --> 00:06:32.039
One is in charge of talking to pilots, the
other is in charge of talking to their counterparts

00:06:32.039 --> 00:06:34.229
at other sectors to coordinate handovers.

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:39.409
Now, in practice, for that London to Frankfurt
flight, before it enters Maastricht’s airspace

00:06:39.409 --> 00:06:44.039
in the Koksy sector, they’ll receive info
on where and at which flight level it will

00:06:44.039 --> 00:06:45.039
arrive.

00:06:45.039 --> 00:06:49.189
They’ll also get info from the flight plan
on where it’s supposed to exit their sector.

00:06:49.189 --> 00:06:53.779
An aircraft also might enter at one altitude
and be planned to exit at another.

00:06:53.779 --> 00:06:58.719
The task is then to safely navigate the aircraft
from the entry point to exit point and deliver

00:06:58.719 --> 00:07:01.050
it to the next sector at the desired altitude.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:06.199
Now, assuming no added obstructions such as
weather or airspace closures, the main obstacles

00:07:06.199 --> 00:07:10.100
planes need to avoid at this altitude are
other planes.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:14.219
There are rules about how close a plane can
be to another in order to avoid any chance

00:07:14.219 --> 00:07:18.289
of midair collision and the controller’s
job is to make sure that these rules are not

00:07:18.289 --> 00:07:23.620
broken or, if they are, to get to the correct
level of separation as soon as possible.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:29.189
An aircraft must be either vertically or horizontally
separated from all others at any given time.

00:07:29.189 --> 00:07:33.119
What this means is that typically, a commercial
aircraft cruising at this altitude can be

00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:38.199
as little as 1000 vertical feet or 300 vertical
meters away from another.

00:07:38.199 --> 00:07:40.289
That’s vertical separation.

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:43.309
Alternatively, an aircraft can be horizontally
separated.

00:07:43.309 --> 00:07:48.729
They have to be at least 5 nautical miles,
6 miles, or 9 kilometers apart if they’re

00:07:48.729 --> 00:07:50.909
within 1000 feet vertically.

00:07:50.909 --> 00:07:55.639
Now, in order to achieve the goals of getting
the aircraft to its exit point without breaking

00:07:55.639 --> 00:08:01.539
minimum separation, there are three factors
the controller can instruct the pilot to change—speed,

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:03.639
altitude, and direction.

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:08.000
That is essentially what a controller spends
most of their time doing—determining where

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:12.860
the aircraft needs to go, how to get it there,
and communicating that to the pilot.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.330
Soon enough, after just a few minutes, as
the aircraft reaches its exit point from the

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:20.840
sector, it will be passed onto the controller
of the next sector.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:25.479
In this case, it’ll go into Nicky Sector,
then Olno sector, then it will move onto airspace

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:28.759
beyond what the Maastricht Upper Area Control
Centre manages.

00:08:28.759 --> 00:08:32.849
As the plane starts to descends it will be
passed onto the controllers dealing with lower

00:08:32.849 --> 00:08:36.519
airspace, then approach and tower control
to guide it into landing.

00:08:36.519 --> 00:08:41.139
All told, on just this hour long flight, more
than a dozen air traffic controllers will

00:08:41.139 --> 00:08:42.450
have dealt with this aircraft.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:47.000
But that’s what happens when absolutely
everything is going right, which is rarely

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.000
the case.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:53.010
You see, back at Eurocontrol’s network manager
room, the second thing they do with the flight

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:57.339
plans they receive is make sure that once
an aircraft gets flying, there are actually

00:08:57.339 --> 00:09:02.730
enough air traffic controllers to manage it.“Thomas:
[00:01:50] So, air traffic control, they decide

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:08.010
on their capacity i.e. how many flights can
safely be handled in one piece of airspace

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:09.959
by one air traffic controller.

00:09:09.959 --> 00:09:17.600
That's their decision based on their staffing,
based on their infrastructure, their tools,

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:19.259
and that is communicated to us.”

00:09:19.259 --> 00:09:23.399
So, there’s a limited number of flights
a single air traffic controller can handle,

00:09:23.399 --> 00:09:26.970
but there’s also a limited number of air
traffic controllers.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:30.899
It’s no secret that Europe, along with much
of the world, is suffering through an air

00:09:30.899 --> 00:09:33.269
traffic controller shortage right now.

00:09:33.269 --> 00:09:37.790
Simultaneously, also along with much of the
world, Europe has been experiencing a tremendous

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:40.410
increase in its number of flights.

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:43.569
This supply and demand mismatch has consequences.

00:09:43.569 --> 00:09:49.270
In 2018, 60% of all en-route delays—as in,
while the aircraft is actually flying—were

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:52.310
because of not having enough air traffic control
capacity.

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:57.360
Part of Eurocontrol’s job, therefore, is
to utilize the limited resource in the most

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:58.990
effective way possible.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:03.910
Often, they reduce overall delays by delaying
flights.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:07.620
Sometimes its just for a few minutes, sometimes
it’s for longer, but if you’re flying

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.930
in Europe and you hear that your flight is
delayed for air traffic control reasons, that

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:14.510
decision was probably made in this room.

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:19.279
It’s just like a ramp meter on a highway—they
let a manageable number of flights fly at

00:10:19.279 --> 00:10:21.750
any given time when there are capacity constraints.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:28.360
Now, when a flight receives a delay by Eurocontrol,
the airline essentially has two choices—accept

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:32.019
the delay and wait it out or fly a different
route.

00:10:32.019 --> 00:10:35.550
Airlines have access to Eurocontrol’s system
to help them make this decision.“Thomas:

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:40.180
[00:09:26] For instance here is a flight,
it's a Thomas Cook flight from Manchester

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:46.170
going to Antalya in Turkey and they have planned
this blue route here.”

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:50.920
This is the route that Thomas Cook has decided
is best for them—it’s the least expensive

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:54.730
considering fuel costs, overflight costs,
and everything.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:58.839
Given that it flies through capacity constrained
areas, though, to fly this route, they would

00:10:58.839 --> 00:11:00.280
have to sit through a delay.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:04.459
“Thomas: [00:10:05] Through our system now
they can look at to see OK where our routes

00:11:04.459 --> 00:11:05.670
that do not give them delay.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:10.279
Those are all the blue routes you can see
here and actually then there is a gray route

00:11:10.279 --> 00:11:12.319
here which is the shortest of the blue.

00:11:12.319 --> 00:11:17.949
A system says if you want to avoid your route
which is delay you can take Blue routes or

00:11:17.949 --> 00:11:21.000
the shortest blue which is the grey and it's
up to you.”

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.970
The airline can then go and decide whether
it’s worth it to them to fly the alternate

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:27.050
route or wait out the delay.

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:31.769
Eurocontrol’s network management role really
is most applicable on the worst days for flying

00:11:31.769 --> 00:11:32.790
in Europe.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:36.290
Their job is to make the bad days as ok as
possible.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:41.839
An example of a really bad day for flying
was Thursday, May 9, 2019.

00:11:41.839 --> 00:11:45.970
The main issue was a large-scale French air
traffic controller strike meaning there was

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:49.910
very little air traffic control capacity in
French airspace—one of the busiest areas.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:56.009
“Celik: [00:03:56] If you look at this map,
that shows the magnitude of the impact on

00:11:56.009 --> 00:11:57.339
the sectors.

00:11:57.339 --> 00:12:04.230
The color red means that there were delays
in the order of 45 minutes or above per flight.”

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:09.000
On days such as this, many airlines will choose
to fly around France as to be less delayed

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:14.269
but, of course, the ATC centers in the surrounding
countries need to be able to cope with demand.

00:12:14.269 --> 00:12:19.949
“Celik: [00:04:30] Starting from three to
four days before this event is confirmed we

00:12:19.949 --> 00:12:27.649
would coordinate with Spain, North African
countries, Algeria, Tunisia, we would coordinate

00:12:27.649 --> 00:12:36.600
with Switzerland, Italy, Germany, Karlsruhe,
we would coordinate with Maastricht and UK

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:42.640
to be able to get extra capacity for all those
who has to go out.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:50.600
So one alternative route from UK to go down
to south of Spain and continuation on that

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.480
what we called Tango route.”

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:57.730
Tango route takes planes way out west into
oceanic airspace to take a wide curve around

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:01.810
French airspace and is commonly flown when
French airspace is restricted.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:03.810
It is, of course, not efficient.

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:08.569
A flight from Alicante to London, for example,
which normally takes just two hours, would

00:13:08.569 --> 00:13:13.410
take over three by using Tango route but overall,
it would typically arrive earlier than if

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:15.970
they waited for approval to fly through French
airspace.

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:21.459
What made the 9th of May even worse is that
drones were spotted at Frankfurt airport meaning

00:13:21.459 --> 00:13:23.680
all arrivals and departures were stopped.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:30.939
“Celik: [00:14:02] What we do we immediately
get in contact with all the airports around

00:13:30.939 --> 00:13:31.939
if you like.

00:13:31.939 --> 00:13:34.660
You know this was the case in Frankfurt.

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:43.009
We contacted Amsterdam, Munich, Paris, Brussels
immediately to try to understand incoming

00:13:43.009 --> 00:13:47.600
flights, so live traffic, where they could
land.”

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:51.600
Eurocontrol will get information from the
surrounding airports on how many diverted

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:56.379
aircraft they can handle and passes this information
onto airlines so they can quickly book a diversion

00:13:56.379 --> 00:13:58.560
slot for their aircraft.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:04.480
All told, there were over 300,000 minutes
of delays on May 9th, but initial calculations

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:08.629
determined that there was the potential for
over 1 million minutes of delays which means

00:14:08.629 --> 00:14:12.800
that Eurocontrol likely succeeded in their
task of improving the day.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:16.889
Eurocontrol’s network manager room is expected
to be quite busy in the coming months.

00:14:16.889 --> 00:14:22.300
Right now, in early June, they already know
summer 2019 is going to be messy.

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:26.680
It’s the busiest period of the year for
flights and there’s actually less air traffic

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:32.399
control capacity than there was in summer
2018 when they had 26 million minutes of delays.

00:14:32.399 --> 00:14:37.410
They fear this summer could be worse but,
they made their action plan months ago, and

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:41.810
will be here nonstop through the coming months
to make the summer travel season as smooth

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:42.920
as it can be.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:47.290
Overall, this room, air traffic control centers,
and the rest of the world’s air traffic

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:52.350
management infrastructure are crucial tools
to make sure that routine, uneventful flights

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:56.680
like London to Frankfurt stay uneventful.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:59.069
One thing that aviation loves is checklists.

00:14:59.069 --> 00:15:03.459
They make sure you complete crucial steps
and that’s why, when I launch something

00:15:03.459 --> 00:15:06.790
new, I have my own checklist of crucial steps.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:10.740
One of the first steps, after coming up with
a name, is buying the domain name.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:15.689
That’s because I know that domains go fast
and, if you wait too long, someone else might

00:15:15.689 --> 00:15:17.180
take your perfect domain.

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:22.600
I recently launched a podcast, for example,
and immediately bought the domain extremitiespodcast.com

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:23.600
through Hover.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:27.720
I haven’t even set up a website yet but,
considering the low price, it was worth it

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:32.389
to me to sit on the domain and, for now, I
took about 30 seconds to set up the domain

00:15:32.389 --> 00:15:35.100
to redirect to the podcast download page.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:39.290
